UNIT ansiRegex
KIND FUNCTIONAL
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
SIG ansiRegex({ onlyFirst = false } = {}) -> RegExp
DEPENDS-ON none

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SUMMARY
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ansiRegex returns a NEW RegExp on every call. The RegExp matches ANSI
# escape codes. The regex SOURCE (pattern string) is a FIXED CONSTANT and is
# IDENTICAL for every call regardless of options. The only thing the option
# controls is the FLAGS of the returned RegExp:
#   - default / falsy onlyFirst -> global flag  'g'
#   - truthy onlyFirst          -> no flags      ''
#
# The unit is a pure value-mode utility. No effects, no ambient reads, no
# clock/RNG/io/net seam. EFFECTS: N/A (read-only / pure). ORACLE-CLASS is
# plain `deterministic` (no seam to inject).
#
# The OUTPUT is a RegExp. The oracle codec round-trips RegExp via its regex
# tag, comparing { source, flags }. Both source and flags are deterministic
# and fully specified below, so the output IS gradable.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SIGNATURE / PARAMETER SEMANTICS  (BRANCH-bearing — read exactly)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The function is an arrow function with a SINGLE parameter that is an object
# with DEFAULT-DESTRUCTURING:
#
#     ({ onlyFirst = false } = {}) => { ... }
#
# Two layers of defaulting, in this precise order:
#
#  (A) Outer default `= {}`:
#      The whole argument defaults to `{}` ONLY when the passed value is
#      `undefined`. This is JS default-parameter semantics: it triggers for
#      `undefined` (including a missing/no-arg call) but NOT for any other
#      value (not for null, not for 0, not for '').
#        - ansiRegex()            -> arg is undefined -> becomes {}   -> OK
#        - ansiRegex(undefined)   -> arg is undefined -> becomes {}   -> OK
#        - ansiRegex(null)        -> arg stays null -> destructuring null THROWS
#                                    a TypeError ("Cannot destructure property
#                                    'onlyFirst' of null as it is null.").
#                                    => THROW PATH. (See ERROR / THROW below.)
#
#  (B) Inner default `onlyFirst = false`:
#      After (A), the object is destructured for property `onlyFirst`. If the
#      property is absent OR explicitly `undefined`, it defaults to `false`.
#        - ansiRegex({})                 -> onlyFirst = false
#        - ansiRegex({ onlyFirst: undefined }) -> onlyFirst = false
#        - any other key(s) are IGNORED (e.g. { foo: 1 } -> onlyFirst = false)
#
# `onlyFirst` is then used as a TERNARY CONDITION (truthiness test), NOT a
# strict `=== true` comparison:
#
#     return new RegExp(pattern, onlyFirst ? undefined : 'g');
#
#   - onlyFirst TRUTHY  (true, 1, "yes", {}, [], any truthy value)
#         -> RegExp constructed with flags argument `undefined`
#         -> resulting RegExp has NO flags -> .flags === ''
#   - onlyFirst FALSY   (false, 0, "", null-as-value, NaN, undefined)
#         -> RegExp constructed with flags argument 'g'
#         -> resulting RegExp has the global flag -> .flags === 'g'
#
#   (Note: an explicit `onlyFirst: null` or `onlyFirst: 0` here is FALSY ->
#    'g'. This is the inner property value being falsy, distinct from the
#    THROW caused by passing `null` as the WHOLE argument in (A).)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BEHAVIOR  (ordered pipeline)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# node-1  Resolve the argument object via outer default `= {}` (only for
#         `undefined`). If the argument is `null`, THROW TypeError (node-6).
# node-2  Destructure `onlyFirst`, defaulting to `false` when absent/undefined.
# node-3  Build the constant `pattern` string (see PATTERN CONSTANT below).
#         This string is built by joining a 2-element array with '|':
#           pattern = [ <PART1>, <PART2> ].join('|')
#         The joined result is the exact PATTERN CONSTANT below.
# node-4  Compute flags = onlyFirst ? undefined : 'g'   (truthiness, node BRANCH)
# node-5  Return `new RegExp(pattern, flags)`.
#           - flags === undefined -> RegExp with .flags === ''  (no flags)
#           - flags === 'g'       -> RegExp with .flags === 'g'
#         A FRESH RegExp object is allocated on every call (NOT a shared
#         singleton). Two calls produce distinct objects but EQUAL .source
#         (and equal .flags for the same option). The codec compares by
#         { source, flags }, so identity does not affect grading.
# node-6  ERROR / THROW: passing `null` (or any non-undefined primitive on
#         which property destructuring fails, e.g. ... it only fails for null
#         and undefined; undefined is handled by the outer default, so in
#         practice the ONLY throwing primitive-arg is `null`) throws a
#         TypeError before any RegExp is built. Recorded by the harness as a
#         {__throw} outcome (TypeError). NOTE: this unit's IN-ENVELOPE inputs
#         are option objects + the no-arg case; the null-throw is documented
#         for completeness but is NOT emitted by genInputs (see SELF-CHECK).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PATTERN CONSTANT  (the regex SOURCE — must be reproduced CHARACTER-FOR-
#                    CHARACTER; this is the #1 divergence risk)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# IMPORTANT TRANSCRIPTION RULE: the following is the LITERAL STRING that is
# passed to `new RegExp(pattern, flags)` AND is exactly equal to the resulting
# RegExp's `.source`. It is a 169-character, pure-ASCII string. Every `\` shown
# below is a LITERAL backslash CHARACTER in the string (e.g. the 6 characters
# backslash, u, 0, 0, 1, B). It is NOT a JS escape that collapses to one byte.
# A blind emitter who constructs the RegExp from a STRING must include these
# backslashes verbatim; if they instead author a /regex literal/ they must
# preserve the same escaping so that the produced .source is byte-identical.
#
# In the original source the pattern is assembled as two alternatives joined
# by '|'. Reproduced here as a JS source-literal expression (the explanatory
# prose uses '#'; the code below is the literal content, not a comment):
#
#     pattern = [
#       '[\\u001B\\u009B][[\\]()#;?]*(?:(?:(?:(?:;[-a-zA-Z\\d\\/#&.:=?%@~_]+)*|[a-zA-Z\\d]+(?:;[-a-zA-Z\\d\\/#&.:=?%@~_]*)*)?\\u0007)',
#       '(?:(?:\\d{1,4}(?:;\\d{0,4})*)?[\\dA-PR-TZcf-ntqry=><~]))'
#     ].join('|')
#
# (Above is rendered as it appears in a JS SINGLE-QUOTED literal: each `\\`
#  there is one literal backslash in the resulting string. So `\\u001B` in the
#  literal becomes the 6-char sequence `\u001B` (backslash,u,0,0,1,B) in the string/.source.)
#
# The RESULTING .source string (the EXACT characters, verbatim, between the
# markers; the markers themselves are not part of it):
#
# >>>[\u001B\u009B][[\]()#;?]*(?:(?:(?:(?:;[-a-zA-Z\d\/#&.:=?%@~_]+)*|[a-zA-Z\d]+(?:;[-a-zA-Z\d\/#&.:=?%@~_]*)*)?\u0007)|(?:(?:\d{1,4}(?:;\d{0,4})*)?[\dA-PR-TZcf-ntqry=><~]))<<<
#
# Anchors (verified by running the real package @ ansi-regex 5.0.1):
#   - .source.length === 169
#   - UTF-8 byte length === 169 (pure ASCII; no multibyte chars)
#   - .source is IDENTICAL for onlyFirst:false and onlyFirst:true (only flags differ)
#   - ansiRegex().source.startsWith('[\u001B\u009B]')   # first 14 chars: bracket, backslash-u-001B, backslash-u-009B, bracket
#   - ansiRegex().source.endsWith('?[\dA-PR-TZcf-ntqry=><~]))')
#   - The two-part array joined by '|' reconstructs the source exactly
#     (verified: parts.join('|') === ansiRegex().source -> true).
#
# Per-character note on the contained ranges/classes (so the emitter does not
# misread the dense character classes):
#   - Outer leading class:  []   (ESC and CSI bytes, as literal \u escapes)
#   - Next class:           [[\]()#;?]       (note the literal '[' inside, then \] for ']')
#   - Sub-classes contain:  [-a-zA-Z\d\/#&.:=?%@~_]   (note \/ for '/', \d for digit)
#   - Final class:          [\dA-PR-TZcf-ntqry=><~]
#       ranges: \d , A-P , R-T , Z , c , f-n , t , q , r , y , and literals = > < ~
#   - Quantifiers: *  +  ?  {1,4}  {0,4}
#   - The  (BEL) terminator is literal backslash-u-0007.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ORACLE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
ORACLE value -> oracle.json
# Output is a RegExp; codec round-trips it via the regex tag => grades on
# { source, flags }. source is the fixed PATTERN CONSTANT for ALL inputs;
# flags is 'g' (default / falsy onlyFirst) or '' (truthy onlyFirst).
# The null-argument throw path is recorded as {__throw: TypeError} if exercised
# (not emitted by the generator — see SCOPE / SELF-CHECK).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SCOPE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# IN-CONTRACT LOGIC (reproduce exactly):
#   - The default-destructuring `({ onlyFirst = false } = {})` semantics
#     (outer `= {}` only for undefined; inner `= false` for absent/undefined key).
#   - The TRUTHINESS-based flags selection `onlyFirst ? undefined : 'g'`.
#   - The FIXED 169-char PATTERN CONSTANT above (the regex .source), reproduced
#     byte-identically.
#   - Returning a fresh `new RegExp(pattern, flags)` each call.
# CARRIED DATA: none. (The pattern is small in-contract code, not a carried table.)
# OUT-OF-CONTRACT: none (no effects, no stateful API, no ambient seam).
# OUT-OF-ENVELOPE (documented, NOT emitted by genInputs):
#   - `ansiRegex(null)` -> throws TypeError (destructuring null). Documented at
#     node-6; not generated because the generator emits only option objects and
#     the no-arg / undefined case (all codec-round-trippable, all non-throwing).
#   - Non-round-trippable arg shapes (functions as the arg, Symbols, getters,
#     boxed primitives) are NOT relevant here and are NOT emitted: every
#     in-envelope arg is either MISSING (no-arg) or a PLAIN OPTION OBJECT whose
#     values are JSON-round-trippable booleans/numbers/strings. The behavior is
#     governed solely by the truthiness of the `onlyFirst` property, which the
#     SIR fully specifies, so the generator confines itself to such values.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SELF-CONSISTENCY CROSS-CHECK (SIR <-> genInputs)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# genInputs emits ONLY:
#   - the no-arg call (empty tuple [])                  -> default {} -> 'g'
#   - [ {} ]                                            -> onlyFirst=false -> 'g'
#   - [ { onlyFirst: false } ]                          -> 'g'
#   - [ { onlyFirst: true } ]                           -> '' (no flags)
#   - [ { onlyFirst: <truthy non-bool> } ]   (e.g. 1, "yes") -> '' (documented truthiness)
#   - [ { onlyFirst: <falsy non-bool> } ]    (e.g. 0, "")    -> 'g' (documented truthiness)
#   - [ { foo: <x>, onlyFirst: <bool> } ]    extra keys ignored
# Every one of those mappings is defined explicitly above (param semantics +
# truthiness BRANCH + fixed PATTERN CONSTANT). The generator emits no value
# whose behavior is left unspecified. The null-throw path is documented but NOT
# generated. SELF-CHECK: PASS.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EMISSION DIRECTIVE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The original is `module.exports = (fn)` (CommonJS default export). Emissions
# are .mjs consumed via import(). The blind re-emitter MUST emit:
#     export default <fn>
# i.e. a single default export that is the ansiRegex function.

PROVENANCE ansi-regex@5.0.1 (chalk/ansi-regex), MIT license, source file index.js
